\f1\fs22 Agora Symposium Bar was exactly the kind of establishment that would make actual ancient Greeks roll in their graves while making modern ones feel smugly educated. The walls were adorned with knock-off friezes displaying athletic competitions that no actual Greek would recognise, alongside misattributed quotes in Comic Sans Greek font. Serving staff wore bedsheet togas pinned with plastic fibulae, and the cocktail menu featured drinks like "Socrates' Hemlock Martini" and "Dionysian Delight."\
None of this bothered the three men reclining on the overstuffed kline couches in the VIP section. They had booked the entire back room under the name "Seven Sages LLC," though they were only three. A small fountain burbled in the center, surrounded by platters of olives, feta, and honey-drizzled figs that none of them had touched.\
"To prophecy," Chilon Damagetus raised his kylix of wine. The stem of the drinking vessel was adorned with a plastic grape cluster that lit up when tilted.\
"To interpretation," countered Solon Athena, his lean frame barely disturbing the purple cushions as he reached for his own drink.\
"To the folly of certainty," Kleoboulos Windus completed the toast, his senior years evident in the controlled precision of his movements.\
They drank in unison, then grimaced simultaneously at the sweetened house wine.\
"Barbaric," muttered Kleo, setting down his cup. "Like drinking a liquefied lollipop."\
"The Greeks watered their wine," Solo reminded him, swirling the offensive liquid. "Though I doubt water would improve this particular vintage."\
Chilo wiped his mouth with the back of his hand. "No matter. We're not here for historical accuracy." He lowered his voice, though the thumping electronic rendition of what the DJ imagined to be ancient lyre music would have drowned out any eavesdroppers. "Project Kebab proceeds exactly as foreseen."\
"Johnny is even more malleable than we anticipated," Solo agreed. "His desperation to impress Sarah makes him extraordinarily suggestible."\
"And the Gyges Discs respond to the pentagram configuration," Chilo continued, a gleam in his eyes that reflected more than just the tacky blue lighting. "The dimensional barriers are already thinning."\
Kleo reached for an olive, examined it critically, then set it aside without eating. "Yet you continue to push beyond what the prophecy requires."\
"I push toward what the prophecy demands," Chilo corrected. "The arrival of the new Dawkins requires the dissolution of the old order."\
"Dissolution, yes. Destruction, no." Solo straightened, abandoning the pretense of relaxed reclining. "You speak of dimensional collapse as if it were desirable rather than catastrophic."\
"What is catastrophe but rapid change?" Chilo's smile was thin. "You've grown soft, Solon. Attached to this flawed reality."\
"And you've grown reckless," Kleo interjected. "The prophecy speaks of transformation, not annihilation. The child is meant to bridge worlds, not destroy them."\
A server approached with a platter of "Aristotelian Appetizers," then wisely retreated upon sensing the tension crackling between the three men.\
"The prophecy is clear to those with courage to read it properly," Chilo said, his voice dropping to a near whisper. "When the discs align at midnight on the solstice, as the new Dawkins emerges from the vessel, the barriers between will dissolve, and what emerges will be\'97"\
"Pure speculation," Kleo interrupted. "You've elevated your interpretation above all others."\
"Perhaps because mine is correct." Chilo's calm was terrifying in its completeness. "The texts from the Ephesian archives were quite specific about the dissolution point."\
"Archives you alone claim to have seen," Solo pointed out. "Convenient."\
Chilo's eyes narrowed. "Are you questioning my integrity?"\
"I'm questioning your objectivity," Solo replied evenly. "Your eagerness for universal reconfiguration blinds you to other possibilities."\
"Such as your milquetoast vision of gradual enlightenment?" Chilo scoffed. "The universe doesn't transform through incremental adjustments. It transforms through cataclysm."\
Kleo set down his cup with deliberate care. "The problem with cataclysm, Chilon, is that it rarely discriminates between what should be preserved and what should be discarded."\
"Nothing in this reality deserves preservation," Chilo declared. "It's fundamentally flawed at the quantum level. The new Dawkins will establish correct physical laws in the reborn universe."\
Solo shook his head. "And what of free will? Of human choice? Your vision offers only predetermined destruction."\
"Free will is an illusion of consciousness," Chilo replied. "One the new reality will dispense with."\
"You speak of godhood," Kleo said softly. "Not evolution."\
The table between them suddenly seemed too small, the room too confined.\
"I speak of necessity," Chilo insisted. "The time for half-measures has passed. When Sarah's waters break, I will ensure the discs are at maximum resonance. The child will be born into a universe of his own making."\
"Not if we stop you," Solo said, abandoning any pretense of collegiality.\
Chilo's laugh was genuine. "And how would you do that? You lack both conviction and authority. Johnny trusts my guidance. The discs respond to my calibrations."\
"We could expose you," Kleo suggested mildly. "Tell Sarah what you truly intend."\
"Sarah barely acknowledges your existence," Chilo dismissed. "Besides, the child within her resonates with my purpose. He wants this as much as I do."\
Solo's expression hardened. "Then we'll have to take more direct action."\
The three men stood simultaneously, the pretense of a casual evening evaporating.\
"You're welcome to try," Chilo said, dropping his napkin onto the electronic table, where it disrupted the holograms of "authentic Greek fire."\
A few patrons glanced over as the three distinguished-looking men moved into the small open space beside their couches. When they began removing their jackets and loosening their ties, the bartender sighed.\
"Not again," she muttered to a server. "Call Poulos from security. The philosophy professors are doing their weird Greco-Roman wrestling thing."\
By the time security arrived, the three men had stripped to their waists. Chilo's surprisingly muscular frame belied his age, while Solo's leaner build suggested speed rather than power. Kleo, despite his years, moved with the assured confidence of someone who had forgotten more about combat than most would ever learn.\
They circled each other in the traditional stance of pankration, a combat sport that combined wrestling and boxing. The patrons, assuming this was part of the bar's entertainment, began cheering and placing bets.\
"Last chance to reconsider your opposition," Chilo offered, hands raised.\
"Last chance to moderate your ambition," Solo countered.\
"Last chance for both of you to recognize your folly," Kleo added.\
For a moment, the only sound was the anachronistic electronic music and the splashing of the small fountain. Then, with a speed that shocked onlookers, all three lunged simultaneously.\
The security footage would later become somewhat of a viral sensation, though viewers would debate whether the three elderly academics were performing some obscure classical demonstration or simply having a senior moment. What was clear, however, was the genuine intensity with which they grappled, as if the fate of reality itself hung in the balance of their contest.\
What was less clear, as the clip ended with the three men being forcibly separated and escorted from the premises, was which of them, if any, had emerged victorious.
